From: Proton CRM <noreply@proton.local>
Subject: [PATCH] P9 task 7: one switch per feature, not two

Closes the two-switch gap recorded twice in the blocked-work register (3g for
`FAQ_SUGGESTION_POPUP_ENABLED`, 3h for `INBOUND_ALERTS_ENABLED`): a feature
gated client-side on `hasFeature('<name>')` AND server-side on a backend
`Settings` flag of the same name, with nothing connecting the two. An operator
flipped the documented switch, saw nothing, and had no way to tell a
mis-configured tenant from a broken feature. It happened twice, so the fix is
mechanical rather than another paragraph of documentation.

WHAT CHANGES. `app/views/layouts/vueapp.html.erb` (0001's own added block) stops
reading `PROTON_FEATURES` as the whole truth and instead builds the feature list
as `PROTON_FEATURES` **plus** the feature name of every backend flag that is set
truthy in the Rails container's environment. `deploy/docker-compose.tenant.yml`
is what puts them there: it already gives the `backend` service the tenant env
file wholesale (`env_file: tenants/${TENANT}.env`), so the same file's
`INBOUND_ALERTS_ENABLED` / `FAQ_SUGGESTION_POPUP_ENABLED` are now also forwarded
into `x-chatwoot-env`, which both `chatwoot-rails` and `chatwoot-sidekiq` use.
One variable in one file, read by both halves of the feature.

WHY THE ERB AND NOT AN ENDPOINT. `useProtonConfig()` calls nothing: it is a
synchronous read of `window.__PROTON_CONFIG__`, which the Rails layout stamps at
render time, and every consumer (`ai_assist`, `nav_menu`, `copilot`,
`knowledge`, `faq_suggestion_popup`, `inbound_alerts`) reads it synchronously at
setup. Making the backend report its enabled features over HTTP would mean
turning that read async and auditing every call site for a load-order race in
which a feature is briefly "off" -- a large change to verify, for a list whose
values are already available at render time. The mapping is one Ruby hash in the
place the list is already built.

ADDITIVE, NEVER SUBTRACTIVE, AND STILL DEFAULT-OFF.
* `PROTON_FEATURES` keeps working unchanged and keeps its compose default
  (`ai_assist,nav_menu,copilot,knowledge`). A tenant that has already added
  `inbound_alerts` by hand keeps it whatever the flag says, so this cannot turn
  a working feature off.
* A flag that is absent or false contributes nothing, so an existing tenant --
  `deploy/tenants/default.env` included, which sets neither flag -- renders a
  byte-identical `features` list to before this patch.
* `<%= %>` HTML-escapes as it did before, and `<script>` is an HTML raw-text
  element, so the escaping posture of the injected string is unchanged.

THE TRUTHY SET COVERS EVERYTHING pydantic-settings ACCEPTS AS TRUE (`1 true t
yes y on`, case-insensitive). If it did not, a tenant writing
`INBOUND_ALERTS_ENABLED=y` would get the backend on and the frontend off -- a
third variant of exactly the state this patch exists to remove. Verified against
the real `Settings` field over a corpus of values, not against pydantic's
documentation, in `test_p9_task7_feature_flag_unification.py`.

The two parsers are not *identical*, and the difference is stated rather than
papered over: this block also trims whitespace, and pydantic does not -- it raises
`ValidationError` on `"  true  "`. So that one value renders the feature on and
stops the backend from starting at all. That is a loud failure on deploy, not a
half-on feature, which is why the trim stays; the invariant that matters and is
asserted is narrower and one-directional: **no value of the variable leaves the
backend enabled while the SPA's gate is shut.** Anything pydantic rejects takes
the backend down with it, so no running system can be in that state.

THE `--env-file` FLAG IS LOAD-BEARING, AND THAT IS THE ONE REMAINING FRAGILITY.
Compose interpolation reads the shell environment and whatever is passed as
`--env-file`; it does NOT read a service's own `env_file:` entries. Deploy a
tenant stack without `--env-file tenants/<tenant>.env` and the backend still sees
the flag while Rails falls back to `:-false` -- the original two-switch bug,
reconstructed. Every script and every README command in this repo passes the
flag, and a test asserts they continue to; but an operator running `docker
compose up` by hand without it gets the old behaviour back with no warning.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO. It does not make `INBOUND_ALERTS_ENABLED` gate the
backend's alert-rule store or its router (`ALERT_RULES_ENABLED` is a separate,
genuinely separate feature and keeps its own switch), and it does not change any
JavaScript: `hasFeature('inbound_alerts')` in `useProtonInboundAlerts.js` and
`hasFeature('faq_suggestion_popup')` in `ReplyTopPanel.vue` are untouched, which
is why 0056's and 0057's tests are unaffected.

DEPENDENCIES / STACKING. Stacks on **0001** only, and on nothing else: 0001 is
the sole patch in the series that touches `app/views/layouts/vueapp.html.erb`,
and the three context lines above the hunk plus the three below are upstream's
own, transcribed verbatim from 0001's merged diff. The hunk was GENERATED BY
`git diff` against a synthetic pre-image built from 0001's post-image, so the
`@@` arithmetic is git's rather than hand-written -- the failure mode that broke
0056's counts.

UNVERIFIED, STATED PLAINLY. This has never been applied to a real Chatwoot
checkout and never been through a Cloud Build; this sandbox has no network access
to clone upstream. Its tests apply it to a synthetic reconstruction and EXECUTE
the shipped ERB fragment through Ruby's own `erb` library, and resolve the real
compose file with the real `docker compose` CLI, so both halves of the chain are
real output rather than a Python re-implementation of them -- but nothing here
proves a browser ever saw `window.__PROTON_CONFIG__`. See
`docs/analysis/2026-08-09-blocked-work-register.md` sections 3g and 3h.

WHAT AN OPERATOR STILL HAS TO DO. The compose passthrough takes effect on the
next `up -d` of the tenant stack; this ERB change only takes effect once the
Chatwoot custom image is REBUILT with this patch in it (Cloud Build, amd64,
off-VM). Until that image is pulled, the live tenants keep two switches and the
env documentation's older warning is still the accurate description of them.
diff --git a/app/views/layouts/vueapp.html.erb b/app/views/layouts/vueapp.html.erb
index 47db376..1b747ea 100644
--- a/app/views/layouts/vueapp.html.erb
+++ b/app/views/layouts/vueapp.html.erb
@@ -64,11 +64,38 @@
       }
       window.errorLoggingConfig = '<%= ENV.fetch('SENTRY_FRONTEND_DSN', '') || ENV.fetch('SENTRY_DSN', '') %>'
     </script>
+    <%
+      # ONE SWITCH PER FEATURE (P9 task 7). Some Proton features are gated on
+      # this list client-side AND on a backend Settings flag of the same name
+      # server-side. Two switches for one feature means an operator flips the
+      # documented one, sees nothing, and reports the feature as broken -- which
+      # has already happened twice (INBOUND_ALERTS_ENABLED and
+      # FAQ_SUGGESTION_POPUP_ENABLED). So the flag env var is the single truth:
+      # docker-compose.tenant.yml passes the SAME variable from
+      # tenants/<tenant>.env to the backend (via env_file) and to Rails (via
+      # x-chatwoot-env), and this block turns it into the feature name the SPA
+      # reads. PROTON_FEATURES still works and is still additive -- a tenant
+      # that already lists a name keeps it, so this never turns anything off.
+      #
+      # The truthy set matches pydantic-settings' own bool parsing, deliberately:
+      # if the two disagreed, a tenant writing INBOUND_ALERTS_ENABLED=y would get
+      # the backend on and the frontend off, which is the very state this exists
+      # to remove.
+      proton_flag_features = {
+        'INBOUND_ALERTS_ENABLED' => 'inbound_alerts',
+        'FAQ_SUGGESTION_POPUP_ENABLED' => 'faq_suggestion_popup',
+      }
+      proton_features = ENV.fetch('PROTON_FEATURES', '').split(',').map { |f| f.strip }.reject { |f| f.empty? }
+      proton_flag_features.each do |flag_name, feature_name|
+        next unless %w[1 true t yes y on].include?(ENV.fetch(flag_name, '').to_s.strip.downcase)
+        proton_features << feature_name unless proton_features.include?(feature_name)
+      end
+    %>
     <script>
       window.__PROTON_CONFIG__ = {
         backendUrl: "<%= ENV.fetch('PROTON_BACKEND_URL', '') %>",
         backendKey: "<%= ENV.fetch('PROTON_BACKEND_KEY', '') %>",
-        features: "<%= ENV.fetch('PROTON_FEATURES', '') %>".split(',').filter(Boolean)
+        features: "<%= proton_features.join(',') %>".split(',').filter(Boolean)
       };
     </script>
     <% if @global_config['CLOUD_ANALYTICS_TOKEN'].present? %>
